be removed if it's the only one). So that descendant would still be present. If we later remove the parent, the descendant is already removed? No, we removed the descendant earlier? Actually we cannot remove the descendant if it's the only required symbol. So it remains. Then if we remove the parent, the descendant becomes orphaned? But the tree structure: if we remove a parent, what happens to its children? The children are no longer connected to root? The problem likely expects that when a node is removed, its entire subtree is removed as well. But the processing order ensures that children are handled first. If a child is retained (because it cannot be removed), then the parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. So we need to check for retained children. So the check for retained children already prevents removing a node that has any retained descendant. So if a required symbol is in a descendant, that descendant is retained (because it was not removable or it's the only instance), so the parent will have retained children. So it will be skipped. So the symbol count check is redundant? But we need to check for required symbols that are not in children but elsewhere. For example, a node with required symbol \"s1\" and there is another node elsewhere also with \"s1\". That node might have no children or its children are all removable/removed. Then it's a leaf. We could remove it if there is another \"s1\" node elsewhere. The tentative check ensures that. So we do both: retained children check and global symbol count check.\n\n10. If all conditions satisfied: remove node from retained_ids, decrement symbol_counts[node.symbol], append node.id to removal_order.\n\n11. After processing all nodes, we have retained_ids set and removal_order list. Return dict: {\"retained_ids\": sorted(retained_ids), \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nBut careful: The order of removal must be the actual order we remove nodes. Since we process in depth descending, that defines removal order. That's fine.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate case input. We can create copies.\n\nTesting with the fixtures: We can mentally simulate for case 0. Nodes: root, n1 (s1, not removable), n2 (s2, removable), n3 (s0, not removable), n4 (s1, removable, depth2), n5 (s2, not removable), n6 (s0, removable). Required symbols: root, s1. Depth order: first depth 2: n4, n5, n6. (IDs: n4, n5, n6; tie break by ID: n4 (1), n5 (2), n6 (3) but IDs are strings, but alphabetical: \"n4\" < \"n5\" < \"n6\"? Actually \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\" lexicographically: \"n4\" < \"n5\" < \"n6\". So order: n4, n5, n6. Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. IDs: n1, n2, n3 => n1, n2, n3. Then root (depth0) but root not removable. So process:\n\n- n4: removable? true. symbol 's1', required, but count of s1 currently: nodes with s1: n1 (depth1) and n4 => count=2. So removal would leave count=1 which is ok. retained children? Check n4's children: none (no nodes with parent n4). So proceed. Remove n4. retained_ids remove n4, removal_order = [n4], symbol_counts s1 becomes 1.\n- n5: removable? false (removable false). So skip.\n- n6: removable? true. symbol 's0', not required. retained children? none. So can remove. Check required symbols: after removal, s0 not required, so fine. Remove n6. retained_ids remove n6, removal_order = [n4, n6].\n- Now depth1: n1: removable? false. skip.\n- n2: removable? true. symbol 's2', not required. retained children? Check children of n2: n4 is child? n4 parent is n2, but n4 is already removed, so no retained children. So can remove. Required symbols: s1 still present at n1. So remove n2. removal_order = [n4, n6, n2]. retained_ids remove n2.\n- n3: removable? false. skip.\n- root: not removable.\nFinal retained_ids: root, n1, n3, n5? Wait n5 is still there (s2, not removable). So retained: [n1, n3, n5, root]. Sorted: [n1, n3, n5, root]? Actually root comes first alphabetically? 'root' vs 'n1'? 'root' > 'n1'? lexicographically 'n1' < 'root' because 'n' < 'r'. So sorted: ['n1', 'n3', 'n5', 'root']? But IDs are strings, so 'n1', 'n3', 'n5', 'root'. But in case output, they might expect something like that. We'll see if that matches expected behavior.\n\nNow, the case also has utility values, but we don't use them. The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That's what we did.\n\nOne nuance: The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That implies descending depth. Good.\n\nAlso, \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrase \"a required symbol\" is ambiguous. Based on the tentative check, we interpret as \"a node whose removal would cause a required symbol to disappear\". But we have the check. I'll stick with the tentative check.\n\nThus, the implementation.\n\nWe need to output valid JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"} with proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe'll include imports if needed? No external libraries. Use standard lib.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nPotential edge: The nodes list might be empty? Not in fixtures. But handle gracefully.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Use copy.deepcopy? Not needed because we are not mutating case but creating new data structures. We'll read nodes and required_symbols directly but not modify original list.\n\n- Building parent_to_children: we can iterate through nodes.\n\n- Sorting: use `sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))`.\n\nProcessing:\n\n```\nretained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\nsymbol_counts = {}\nfor node in nodes:\n    symbol_counts[node['symbol']] = symbol_counts.get(node['symbol'], 0) + 1\nrequired_set = set(case['payload']['required_symbols'])\nremoval_order = []\nfor node in sorted_nodes:\n    nid = node['id']\n    if nid not in retained:\n        continue\n    if not node['removable']:\n        continue\n    # Check if node is root? root is not removable anyway, but also check id not root? root id is \"root\".\n    if nid == 'root':  # root is not removable, but check anyway\n        continue\n    # Check retained children: any child of this node that is still retained?\n    children_ids = parent_to_children.get(nid, [])\n    if any(cid in retained for cid in children_ids):\n        continue\n    # Check if removing would cause required symbol missing\n    # If node's symbol is required and its count is 1, cannot remove\n    sym = node['symbol']\n    if sym in required_set and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\n        continue\n    # Remove\n    retained.remove(nid)\n    symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n    removal_order.append(nid)\nreturn {\"retained_ids\": sorted(retained), \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n```\n\nWe should also consider that after removing a node, its children are implicitly removed? They are not in retained because we remove them individually if they were removed earlier. But if a child was not processed yet because it's deeper? That's not possible because deeper processed first. But what if a node has a child that is deeper but we skip it because it's not removable? That child would be retained later, but then the parent would have retained child and thus cannot be removed. So it's fine.\n\nAlso, note: The parent_to_children should be built from the original tree. Since we don't remove children from this mapping, it's static. The check for retained children uses the child list from original tree. That's correct.\n\nOne more thing: The root's children are n1, n2, n3. After removing n2, the parent_to_children of root still lists n2, but we check if any child is retained. Since n2 is removed, it's not in retained, so root can be removed? But root is not removable and not required? Actually root is required and not removable. So it's fine. But if root were removable, this logic would allow removing root after all its children are removed. But root is never removable. So ok.\n\nNow, produce the JSON.\n\nWe need to escape the Python source string. Write it as a single line with newlines escaped as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". We'll create a multiline string and then apply JSON encoding. In the final output, it should be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being that string.\n\nWe'll write the Python code and then use json.dumps to escape, but since we are generating JSON manually, we need to escape ourselves. We'll do it carefully.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll construct the string using Python's json.dumps on the code string. But since this response must be exactly the JSON object, I can compute it. I'll write a Python script mentally and produce the escaped string.\n\nAlternatively, I can write the code in a raw string and then manually escape. Better to produce the final JSON.\n\nLet me write the solve function as a string:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    parent_children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            parent_children.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        symbol_count[n['symbol']] = symbol_count.get(n['symbol'], 0) + 1\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        children = parent_children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(c in retained for c in children):\n            continue\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    return {\n        'retained_ids': sorted(retained),\n        'removal_order': removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nNow, we need to escape this string for JSON. We'll replace backslashes with double backslashes, newlines with \\n, quotes with \\\". Also, we need to ensure the string is correctly formatted.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON string manually.\n\nI'll compute the escaped version:\n\nOriginal code as a single string with newlines:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    parent_children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            parent_children.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        symbol_count[n['symbol']] = symbol_count.get(n['symbol'], 0) + 1\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        children = parent_children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(c in retained for c in children):\n            continue\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    return {\n        'retained_ids': sorted(retained),\n        'removal_order': removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nNow replace:\n\n- backslashes: none in code except maybe in string quotes? No.\n- newlines: replace each newline with \\n.\n- double quotes: we have single quotes in Python code, but inside the JSON string we will use double quotes for the outer string. So single quotes in Python are fine, no need to escape. But we must escape any double quotes if they appear. There are no double quotes in this code. So we just replace newlines with \\n.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure indentation is preserved. We'll represent the newline as \\n and then the next line continuation.\n\nSo the escaped string will be: `def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    ...` etc.\n\nLet's write it out:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    parent_children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            parent_children.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        symbol_count[n['symbol']] = symbol_count.get(n['symbol'], 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        children = parent_children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    return {\\n        'retained_ids': sorted(retained),\\n        'removal_order': removal_order\\n    }\n```\n\nWe also need to escape backslash if any, but none.\n\nNow, construct the JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"...\"} with that string.\n\nDouble-check that we included node_map but never used it. That's fine; it's unused but not harmful. We can remove it to make code cleaner, but it's okay. However, the problem says \"may use only the Python standard library.\" No restrictions on unused variables. I'll remove node_map to avoid clutter.\n\nAlso, ensure we handle case where payload may have missing keys? But fixtures have them.\n\nThus, final code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n    parent_children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            parent_children.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        symbol_count[n['symbol']] = symbol_count.get(n['symbol'], 0) + 1\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        children = parent_children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(c in retained for c in children):\n            continue\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    return {\n        'retained_ids': sorted(retained),\n        'removal_order': removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nNow escape and put in JSON.\n\nI'll produce the final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1734,"completion_tokens":6802,"total_tokens":8536,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":6461},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":70},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}